1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,314
An agent asks to issue a refund. A policy decides whether that request is allowed.

2
00:00:07,564 --> 00:00:13,292
But before changing the policy, how do we know which recorded requests would change?

3
00:00:13,542 --> 00:00:20,710
ScopeTwin makes that impact visible, with deterministic evaluation, frozen replay, and
human review.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,128
This is the real application, running locally with synthetic data in an isolated
workspace.

5
00:00:29,378 --> 00:00:35,302
The published rules allow refunds up to five hundred dollars and require review above
that amount.

6
00:00:35,552 --> 00:00:42,645
Amounts use integer cents. Unknown actions are denied, and a matching deny takes
precedence.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:49,299
We now send three actual requests to the application API.

8
00:00:49,549 --> 00:00:59,236
A one hundred and twenty dollar refund is allowed. A twenty four hundred dollar refund
requires review. An unknown action is denied.

9
00:00:59,486 --> 00:01:07,249
Each result records its reason and the published policy snapshot. No refund or
external action is executed.

10
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:17,073
Next, we propose a stricter boundary: deny refunds above one hundred dollars.

11
00:01:17,323 --> 00:01:23,987
This is an unpublished draft. Saving it does not change the rules used by live
requests.

12
00:01:24,237 --> 00:01:31,829
Preview impact runs the same deterministic engine over our three recorded inputs,
without calling an external service.

13
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,778
The Replay Lab reports exactly two changed decisions and two newly denied requests.

14
00:01:45,028 --> 00:01:53,561
It keeps three facts separate: the original recorded outcome, the frozen published
baseline, and the candidate simulation.

15
00:01:53,811 --> 00:02:00,295
These are measured label changes. They are not claims about money saved or losses
prevented.

16
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,183
Here is the individual comparison for one recorded request.

17
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:13,947
The original and baseline remain intact, while the candidate explains its matching
rule and reason.

18
00:02:14,197 --> 00:02:20,040
Frozen inputs and policy definitions make this evidence reproducible after later
edits.

19
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:27,878
A review outcome creates a human approval request with a freshness boundary.

20
00:02:28,128 --> 00:02:34,882
We open the confirmation dialog, cancel once, then record approval through the real
interface.

21
00:02:35,132 --> 00:02:43,574
Approval documents a human choice. It preserves the original review decision and
grants no permission to execute a refund.

22
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:52,649
The audit trail is also real application evidence.

23
00:02:52,899 --> 00:03:01,692
This scoped audit endpoint returns the stored event for our synthetic evaluation, with
safe metadata and an authenticated workspace boundary.

24
00:03:01,942 --> 00:03:06,705
It is a JSON API view, not a separate audit dashboard.

25
00:03:08,705 --> 00:03:13,364
Usage and Plans derives its counts from actual workspace records.

26
00:03:13,614 --> 00:03:22,222
Our three evaluations, replay, and approval are visible here. Pricing is a proposal,
with no checkout or charges.

27
00:03:22,472 --> 00:03:28,950
Every usage count comes from workspace records, and replay never executes an external
action.

28
00:03:33,325 --> 00:03:38,914
ScopeTwin is one Laravel, React, and PostgreSQL application.

29
00:03:39,164 --> 00:03:46,362
Explore the read only synthetic tour, then evaluate, replay, and review in your own
workspace.

30
00:03:46,612 --> 00:03:48,981
Execution stays with you.
